{"name":"Terra Earth","hex":"#d59982","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"terra","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A geologic and geographic region of a celestial body which is a highland analogous to a continent or large landmass."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Earth, soil, land, or ground as a physical surface.","The material world as opposed to the heavens."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/terra"},{"term":"earth","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["Alternative letter-case form of Earth (“the third planet of the Solar System, upon which humans live”)."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Soil.","Any general rock-based material."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/earth"}],"entries":[{"title":"Terra (mythology)","blocks":[{"text":"In ancient Roman religion and mythology, Tellus, Terra, Tellus Mater or Terra Mater is the personification of the Earth. Although Tellus and Terra are hardly distinguishable during the Imperial era, Tellus was the name of the original earth goddess in the religious practices of the Republic or earlier. The scholar Varro (1st century BC) lists Tellus as one of the di selecti, the twenty principal gods of Rome, and one of the twelve agricultural deities. She is regularly associated with Ceres in rituals pertaining to the earth and agricultural fertility."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4e/Aion_mosaic_Glyptothek_Munich_W504.jpg/330px-Aion_mosaic_Glyptothek_Munich_W504.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Terra_(mythology)","wikidata":"Q270867"},{"title":"Terra (satellite)","blocks":[{"text":"Terra is a multi-national scientific research satellite operated by NASA in a Sun-synchronous orbit around the Earth. It takes simultaneous measurements of Earth's atmosphere, land, and water to understand how Earth is changing and to identify the consequences for life on Earth. It is the flagship of the Earth Observing System (EOS) and the first satellite of the system which was followed by Aqua and Aura. Terra was launched in 1999."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/92/Terra_spacecraft_model.png/330px-Terra_spacecraft_model.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Terra_(satellite)","wikidata":"Q584697"},{"title":"Earth","blocks":[{"text":"Earth is the third planet from the Sun and the only astronomical object known to harbor life. This is made possible by Earth being an ocean world, the only one in the Solar System sustaining liquid surface water. Almost all of Earth's water is contained in its ocean, which covers 70.8% of Earth's crust. The remaining 29.2% of Earth's crust is dry land, which is predominantly located within Earth's land hemisphere in the form of continental landmasses. Most of Earth's land is at least somewhat humid and covered by vegetation, while large ice sheets at Earth's polar deserts retain more water than Earth's groundwater, lakes, rivers, and atmospheric water combined. Earth's crust consists of slowly moving tectonic plates, which interact to produce mountain ranges, volcanoes, and earthquakes. Earth has a liquid outer core that generates a magnetosphere capable of deflecting most of the destructive solar winds and cosmic radiation."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2d/Meteosat-12-fci-march-equinox-2025-noon.jpg/330px-Meteosat-12-fci-march-equinox-2025-noon.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Earth","wikidata":"Q2"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ac/Roman_-_Stele_-_Walters_23184.jpg/500px-Roman_-_Stele_-_Walters_23184.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ac/Roman_-_Stele_-_Walters_23184.jpg/500px-Roman_-_Stele_-_Walters_23184.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A dedicatory inscription to Terra Mater fulfilling a vow (votum), 1st century CE.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ARoman_-_Stele_-_Walters_23184.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Sarcophagus_Dionysos_Met_55.11.5_n08.jpg/500px-Sarcophagus_Dionysos_Met_55.11.5_n08.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Sarcophagus_Dionysos_Met_55.11.5_n08.jpg/1280px-Sarcophagus_Dionysos_Met_55.11.5_n08.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Sarcophagus_Dionysos_Met_55.11.5_n08.jpg/500px-Sarcophagus_Dionysos_Met_55.11.5_n08.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Detail from a sarcophagus depicting a Mother Earth figure (3rd century AD).","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASarcophagus_Dionysos_Met_55.11.5_n08.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8b/Tellus_-_Ara_Pacis.jpg/500px-Tellus_-_Ara_Pacis.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8b/Tellus_-_Ara_Pacis.jpg/1280px-Tellus_-_Ara_Pacis.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8b/Tellus_-_Ara_Pacis.jpg/500px-Tellus_-_Ara_Pacis.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The attributes of the central figure on this panel of the Ara Pacis mark her as an earth and mother goddess, often identified as Tellus.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ATellus_-_Ara_Pacis.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commo